e Carl R. Ice College of Engineering is located on the main Kansas State University campus in Manhattan, Kansas, a vibrant, family-friendly city located in the scenic Flint Hills. Manhattan is the cultural and business hub of a tri-county area community of over 200,000 residents (for more information, see www.manhattan.org ). Worksite Description This position is On-site . All employees must reside in the United States when they begin working to comply with state law. K-State is unable to provide remote or hybrid work opportunities for residents of the state of Idaho. What You'll Need to Succeed Minimum Qualifications: PhD in Nuclear Engineering, Physics, or a closely related field, from an accredited institution The degree requirement is necessary for accreditation purposes and to ensure candidates possess the specialized knowledge, skills, and abilities that can only be attained through a formal educational program at this level. Preferred Qualifications: PhD in Nuclear Engineering with research background in fusion Ability to develop strong externally funded research program in areas relevant to fusion Strong existing National Laboratory collaboration ties Additional Role Information: Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, and continue through the application deadline of November 15, 2026. Sponsorship eligibility: Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the U.S. on an ongoing basis without sponsorship. How to Apply Please submit the following documents: Letter of application, CV, Statement of research, Statement of teaching, and Contact information of five professional references. Application Window Applications close on: November 15, 2026 Anticipated Hiring Pay Range $125,000 - $175,000 annual salary Sign up for job alerts First Name (required) 58de37e3 Last Name (required) 993d39e3 Email (required) 59c88354 Select one or more from the lists below to receive information about specific jobs: 8393a096 Finalize your job alert by selecting cr